XERJ
====

Maintainer notes for the initial OpenBSD port.  This file is not
installed.

Upstream
--------

- Project: https://github.com/xerj-org/xerj
- Selected tag: v1.0.0-rc.100 (prerelease)
- Commit: 682d7ce072c644239cfa13ca1a9ca9a0e45d794b
- License: Apache-2.0
- Workspace: engine/Cargo.toml
- Installed package: xerj-server, binary `xerj`
- Package name: `xerj-1.0.0rc100`; the hyphen in the upstream tag is
  not valid in an OpenBSD package version.
- Architectures: `ONLY_FOR_ARCHS = amd64 aarch64`, matching upstream's
  release matrix.  Other Rust-capable OpenBSD architectures are not
  claimed until built and tested.

No stable v1.0.0 tag was available at the time of writing; the newest
published tag was v1.0.0-rc.100 (2026-10-27).  This port intentionally
pins that tag instead of following main.

Build selection
---------------

This scaffold builds only `xerj-server` and installs only the `xerj`
binary:

    WRKSRC = ${WRKDIST}/engine
    MODCARGO_BUILD_ARGS += --package xerj-server --bin xerj
    MODCARGO_INSTALL_TARGET_PATHS = crates/xerj-server

The upstream default features `neural` and `decide-local` are kept, as in
the upstream release artifact.  They add Candle/tokenizers and the local
decide head, but do not download anything at startup: the default
embedding mode is lexical unless the administrator explicitly selects
`--embed-mode neural` or configures a proxy endpoint.  The neural model
is fetched only when that runtime mode is selected.  The
`onnx-experimental` and `debug-profiling` features are not enabled.
If the Candle/tokenizers stack cannot be built on OpenBSD, the documented
fallback is `MODCARGO_NO_DEFAULT_FEATURES=Yes`; that removes the neural
and local-decide functionality and must be reported as a feature loss,
not as an equivalent package.

`xerj-console-api` unconditionally depends on the Rust `openssl` crate.
The Cargo module's `openssl-sys` handling sets `OPENSSL_NO_VENDOR=1`;
the Makefile repeats it.  The build should therefore use the base-system
LibreSSL headers and libraries, not a vendored OpenSSL.

Native dependencies and patches
-------------------------------

- Base LibreSSL: `crypto` and `ssl` in WANTLIB.  No security/openssl
  dependency is declared.
- SQLite: `xerj-autoindex` enables `rusqlite/bundled`, while the Cargo
  module removes the bundled SQLite sources.  The port patch drops the
  `bundled` feature and uses `databases/sqlite3`.
- zstd: the Cargo module rewrites `zstd-sys` to link the system library;
  `archivers/zstd` is a LIB_DEPENDS and `zstd` is in WANTLIB.  The
  module's generated `-lzstd` has no search path, so
  `MODCARGO_RUSTFLAGS` adds `-L${LOCALBASE}/lib`.
- jemalloc: `tikv-jemalloc-sys` builds its vendored C sources and invokes
  `gmake` for OpenBSD, so `devel/gmake` is a BUILD_DEPENDS.
- ring, tree-sitter and the tree-sitter grammars compile C/assembly
  through their normal build scripts.  No OpenBSD-specific patch has been
  added without evidence.

Generated files that are deliberately absent
--------------------------------------------

The following must be generated on an OpenBSD system before the port can
fetch or build:

    cd /usr/ports/devel/xerj
    make makesum
    make modcargo-gen-crates
    make makesum
    make checksum
    make update-plist

Their absence is expected in this static scaffold:

- `crates.inc`: no MODCARGO_CRATES list has been invented.
- `distinfo`: no source checksum has been invented.
- `pkg/PLIST`: no installed file list has been invented.

The Makefile includes `crates.inc` only when it exists, so the scaffold is
parseable but not yet buildable.

Known runtime risks
-------------------

- The server restores `SIG_DFL` so CLI pipelines into `head` do not panic
  and dump core.  On OpenBSD the standard library/mio does not set
  `SO_NOSIGPIPE`, and a vectored socket write cannot carry
  `MSG_NOSIGNAL`; a peer reset during such a write could terminate the
  server.  The existing tests cover the stdout-pipe path, not this server
  write path.  Reproduce with an RST mid-write before adding a patch.
- Without `/proc` or `/sys`, the memory/RSS probes return no value.  The
  memory governor can fall back to an assumed 8 GiB limit and some
  ES-compatible memory/load metrics are zero.  Set explicit limits in
  the configuration when running on OpenBSD.

Runtime notes
-------------

- Default listener: loopback `127.0.0.1`, ports 8080 (native REST),
  8081 (gRPC) and 9200 (Elasticsearch-compatible).
- Authentication is enabled by default and TLS is disabled by default.
  The default bind is loopback.  Do not ship a configuration that
  disables authentication or exposes a cleartext non-loopback bind;
  provide real certificate paths before enabling a network listener.
- The data directory must be supplied by the administrator.  A future
  rc.d service should use a dedicated unprivileged user and a directory
  under /var; none is added in this initial port because runtime
  persistence, permissions and pledge/unveil behavior have not been
  tested.
- The example configuration is installed as
  `${PREFIX}/share/examples/xerj/xerj.toml`.
- Neural models are downloaded at runtime only when the administrator
  selects the neural embedding mode; the default mode is lexical.

Static verification and pending work
------------------------------------

The upstream tree was inspected, not built.  The following remain
unverified and must be done on OpenBSD:

- The two-stage `make makesum` / `make modcargo-gen-crates` /
  `make makesum` sequence and review of the generated crate list.
- `make checksum`.
- `make patch` and confirmation that every patch applies without fuzz.
- `make build` with the base Rust toolchain.
- Resolution of compile/link failures in native crates and OpenBSD cfg
  branches.
- Run `make test` for the `xerj-server` unit and integration tests.  They
  use loopback listeners and temporary directories, with a writable HOME
  under `${WRKSRC}`.  The separate `xerj-autoindex` test module still
  references the Linux-only `RLIMIT_AS`; that crate's tests are not
  selected by `--package xerj-server` and must be gated before running
  workspace-wide tests.
- `make fake`, `make update-plist`, `make port-lib-depends-check` and
  `make package`.
- Runtime checks for startup, TLS/auth defaults, data directory
  permissions, signal handling and any future pledge/unveil work.
